5. find() in JavaScript

The find() method is used to find the first element in an array that satisfies a condition.

Syntax
array.find(callback);


The callback receives:

element — current element
index — current index (optional)
array — original array (optional)
Example 1: Find a number
const numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40];

const result = numbers.find(num => num > 25);

console.log(result); // 30


find() stops as soon as it finds the first matching element.

Example 2: Find an object
const users = [
  { id: 1, name: "John" },
  { id: 2, name: "Alice" },
  { id: 3, name: "Bob" }
];

const user = users.find(user => user.id === 2);

console.log(user);
// { id: 2, name: "Alice" }

If nothing is found

find() returns undefined.

const numbers = [1, 2, 3];

const result = numbers.find(num => num > 10);

console.log(result); // undefined

find() vs filter()
const numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40];

numbers.find(num => num > 20);
// 30


Returns the first matching element.

numbers.filter(num => num > 20);
// [30, 40]


Returns all matching elements.

Easy way to remember:
find() → find ONE matching element.
filter() → filter ALL matching elements.



