some() — Short explanation

some() checks whether at least one element in an array satisfies a condition.

It returns true or false.

const numbers = [1, 3, 5, 8];

const result = numbers.some(num => num % 2 === 0);

console.log(result);
// true


Why? Because 8 is even.

Real-world example
const users = [
    { name: "John", age: 25 },
    { name: "Alex", age: 17 }
];

const hasMinor = users.some(user => user.age < 18);

console.log(hasMinor);
// true


Remember:

some() → "Is there at least ONE?"

some() → at least one matches → true
every() → all match → true










===========================================
every() — Short explanation

every() checks whether all elements in an array satisfy a condition.

It returns true or false.

const numbers = [2, 4, 6, 8];

const result = numbers.every(num => num % 2 === 0);

console.log(result);
// true


All numbers are even, so it returns true.

Real-world example
const users = [
    { name: "John", age: 25 },
    { name: "Alex", age: 30 }
];

const allAdults = users.every(user => user.age >= 18);

console.log(allAdults);
// true


Remember:

some()  → Is there at least ONE?
every() → Are ALL of them?



===========================================
includes() — Short explanation

includes() checks whether an array or string contains a specific value.

It returns true or false.

const fruits = ["apple", "banana", "mango"];

console.log(fruits.includes("banana"));
// true

console.log(fruits.includes("orange"));
// false

Real-world example
const allowedRoles = ["admin", "manager", "editor"];

const userRole = "manager";

console.log(allowedRoles.includes(userRole));
// true


Remember:

includes() → "Does it contain this value?"


For strings:

const name = "JavaScript";

console.log(name.includes("Script"));
// true



