For basic strings: fruits.sort();


fruits.sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b)) localeCompare() specifically need locale/language-aware string comparison.


1. Basic strings → sort()

Definition:
sort() arranges strings in their default lexicographical (alphabetical-like) order.

const fruits = ["banana", "apple", "orange"];

fruits.sort();

console.log(fruits);
// ["apple", "banana", "orange"]

2. Language-aware strings → sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b))

Definition:
localeCompare() compares two strings according to language/locale rules, and sort() uses that comparison to arrange the strings.

const words = ["éclair", "apple", "banana"];

words.sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b));


Useful when sorting involves different languages, accents, or special characters.

3. Numbers → sort((a, b) => a - b)

Definition:
The comparison function tells sort() to arrange numbers based on their numeric value, from smallest to largest.

const numbers = [10, 2, 5, 1];

numbers.sort((a, b) => a - b);

console.log(numbers);
// [1, 2, 5, 10]


Without (a, b) => a - b, JavaScript treats the numbers like strings when sorting by default.

🧠 Easy to remember
sort()
→ Basic/default string sorting

localeCompare()
→ Language-aware string comparison

a - b
→ Numeric ascending comparison





